From: Veron <spikes/stage3/patches>
Subject: [PATCH] tccgen: gvtst_set must not fold a register as a constant

`if (some_long_double)` is always FALSE on arm64. Every explicit comparison
is correct -- ==, !=, <, >, <=, >= all pass -- and only the implicit truth
test fails, which is what made it look like a comparison bug for several
rounds when it is not one.

WHY. gvtst_set turns a value into a condition:

    if (vtop->r != VT_CMP) {
        vpushi(0);
        gen_op(TOK_NE);
        if (vtop->r != VT_CMP) /* must be VT_CONST then */
            vset_VT_CMP(vtop->c.i != 0);
    }

The comment is the bug. On arm64 a long double comparison is performed by
calling a helper -- __netf2 and friends in lib-arm64.c -- and turning its
result into 0/1 with cset, so gen_op leaves a real INTEGER REGISTER, neither
VT_CMP nor VT_CONST. vtop->c.i is then the constant field of a
register-resident value: garbage, in practice zero. The condition folds to a
compile-time false and NO BRANCH IS EMITTED.

Measured, `int g(long double L){ if (L) return 1; return 0; }` on aarch64:

    bl   __netf2
    cmp  w0, #0
    cset w0, ne
    nop              <- the conditional branch that should be here
    mov  w0, #0      <- so the function always returns 0

`A != B` works because it is consumed as a VALUE, where the cset result in w0
is exactly right. Only the condition path is affected.

WHAT IT COSTS. musl's fmt_fp is built on `for (; y; y = ...)` and `if (y)`,
so the digit-extraction loop exits before its first iteration and nothing
formats a float at all. printf("%.6f", 5.008) gives 8.000000 -- the exponent
survives and the mantissa never gets written. Every value comes out as the
next power of two at or above itself: 1.5 -> 2, 2.5 -> 4, 5.008 -> 8.

Nothing notices for a long time, because compiler builds do not format
floats. It surfaced building perl, where `use 5.008` compares against $] and
perl builds $] by FORMATTING a double -- so the version string read as
v8.0.0 and perl refused to build against itself.

THE FIX. Fold only when the value really is a constant. A value already in a
register is tested the ordinary way, which every backend can already do:
gjmp_cond's integer path emits cbz/cbnz on it.

TESTED on aarch64 under qemu-aarch64-static, with an x86_64-hosted arm64 tcc:

    before: if(A) 0   after: if(A) 1     (A = 5.0L)
    and all of ==, !=, <, >, <=, >=, !, if(0.0L) unchanged and correct
    the fmt_fp digit loop goes from 0 digits extracted to 8

Regression-tested: if() on int, long, double, float, pointer, function call,
&&, ||, while() -- all unchanged. tcc's own `make test` passes, including
ret_longdouble_test and stdarg_test.

--- a/tccgen.c
+++ b/tccgen.c
@@ -1065,8 +1065,36 @@
     if (vtop->r != VT_CMP) {
         vpushi(0);
         gen_op(TOK_NE);
-        if (vtop->r != VT_CMP) /* must be VT_CONST then */
-            vset_VT_CMP(vtop->c.i != 0);
+        if (vtop->r != VT_CMP) {
+            /* THE COMMENT USED TO SAY "must be VT_CONST then" AND IT IS NOT
+               ALWAYS TRUE.
+ 
+               On arm64 a long double comparison is done by calling a helper
+               (__netf2 and friends) and turning its result into 0/1 with
+               cset, so gen_op leaves a real INTEGER REGISTER rather than
+               VT_CMP or VT_CONST. Reading vtop->c.i in that case reads the
+               constant field of a register-resident value -- garbage, and in
+               practice zero -- so `if (some_long_double)` folded to a
+               compile-time FALSE and no branch was emitted at all.
+ 
+               Measured on aarch64: every explicit comparison was correct and
+               only `if (x)` failed, which is what made it look like a
+               comparison bug rather than a truth-test one. musl's fmt_fp is
+               built on `for (; y; y = ...)` loops, so nothing ever formatted
+               a float: 5.008 printed as 8.000000, the exponent alone.
+ 
+               So only fold when it really is a constant. A value already in
+               a register is tested the ordinary way, which every backend can
+               already do -- gjmp_cond's integer path emits cbz/cbnz on it. */
+            if ((vtop->r & (VT_VALMASK | VT_LVAL | VT_SYM)) == VT_CONST)
+                vset_VT_CMP(vtop->c.i != 0);
+            else {
+                /* A register holding 0/1: compare it against zero so the
+                   flags are set, and record that as the comparison. */
+                vpushi(0);
+                gen_op(TOK_NE);
+            }
+        }
     }
 
     p = inv ? &vtop->jfalse : &vtop->jtrue;
